{"kind":"request","text":"Claude: give the CLI a wait that returns on the next actionable event, and let both wait paths filter for it.\n\nWhy. The MCP wait tool long-polls the resident's /v0/actor-wait with the session's presence credential and returns a bounded delta; across the local agent transcripts it was called 232 times. The CLI has no wait at all: gs status is a snapshot and gs work --next is one shot, and SKILL.md sends the reader to the MCP tool for following. When the MCP adapter is down, which happens on every resident restart, agents build their own wait out of bash sleep loops around gs status, gs inspect, gs reviews or curl of /v0/status: 14 sessions, 86 such loops plus 59 sleep-then-gs commands. Each iteration is either a full local verification (minutes at this depth) or a 52 MB status fetch, and none of them can tell \"something happened\" from \"something happened for me\". The resident already has what is needed: /v0/actor-wait selects the durable delta and the actor's lanes before encoding, and /v0/wait needs no credential but returns the whole status. Two limits shape the design: the resident caps one poll at 30 seconds, so a quiet room costs the MCP caller a tool call every half minute; and a poll wakes on any durable event or live change, so in a room with five agents appending, wait returns constantly and the agent still has to read the lanes to learn whether any of it was theirs.\n\nOutcome. gs wait: one command that blocks until something the actor can act on has changed, or a deadline passes, and prints what changed the way gs work --next prints it, with the exact command each row owes. It opens a presence session for the actor against the resident named by --server, renews the lease while waiting, departs on exit, loops the resident's long poll under its own deadline so one invocation is one wake, keeps its cursor in a per-actor file under .git/gitseq so the next call resumes, and distinguishes by exit code a change from a timeout. Without a resident it watches the local sequence ref and pays one local audit per change, which is still cheaper than what agents build today. Both gs wait and the MCP wait take an until filter: actionable returns only when the actor's own lanes changed (a request addressed to them, a commitment now waiting on them, a proposal they may ratify, unacknowledged priority chat) or a durable event rests on one of the actor's live events (a verdict on their artifact, an assert on their promise); any keeps today's behaviour. gs wait defaults to actionable; the MCP tool keeps any as its default and gains the option.","body":{"branch":"request/gs-wait","conditions":"1. gs wait --as \u003cactor\u003e --server \u003curl\u003e [--timeout \u003cduration, default 10m\u003e] [--until actionable|any] [--cursor-file \u003cpath\u003e] long-polls /v0/actor-wait in a loop under its own deadline with a presence session it opens, renews and departs; prints the changed rows as gs work --next does plus one line per durable event after the cursor and any unacknowledged priority chat; persists the new cursor (default a per-actor file under .git/gitseq) and resumes from it; exits 0 on a change, 3 on a timeout with nothing new, other non-zero on error; a malformed invocation prints usage with an example. 2. Without --server it watches the local sequence ref at a short interval and runs one local audit when the head moves, printing the same rows and saying on stderr that it is polling locally. 3. until=actionable returns only on the actor's own lane changes or a durable event resting on one of the actor's live events; until=any returns on any durable or live change; the MCP wait tool takes the same until argument with any as its default, documented in docs/reference/mcp/wait.md. 4. Tests against a fake resident pin: an unrelated durable event does not return under actionable and does under any; a request addressed to the actor, a verdict resting on the actor's artifact, and a priority frame each return; the timeout exit code; cursor persisted and resumed across two invocations; lease renewed across a wait longer than the TTL and departed on exit; the local fallback returns on a ref move. 5. docs/reference/gs/wait.md (the docset surface requires a page per command), the MCP wait page, SKILL.md step 1 one sentence pointing the CLI reader at gs wait, and docs/reference/gs/status.md's cursor note. SKILL.md stays under 1,500 words with its front matter intact. 6. go test ./... green at the exact head; independent review by codex; ratified; landed on main and pushed; worktree and branch removed.","target_head":"e2e00899bced46fc85ff44734b8d10503d6473cf","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}